  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current implementation primarily relies on local optimization techniques (gradient-based methods and simulated annealing) which tend to get stuck in local optima. The central idea is to combine global optimization (Genetic Algorithm) with local refinement. We'll use a multi-resolution approach: start with lower resolutions to escape local optima, then progressively refine higher resolutions. GA handles the global exploration while gradient-based methods handle local exploitation. The approach addresses the non-convex nature of the problem and the challenge of efficiently evaluating the objective function.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe represent h as a real-valued vector where each element 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1.0 and the sum of h equals exactly n_points/2. The objective is to minimize the maximum cross-correlation between h and (1-h), scaled by (2/n_points). The GA will search the space [0,1]^{n_points} subject to the equality constraint. Repair mechanisms are used to maintain feasibility.\n\nImplementation procedure:\nThe algorithm consists of these stages:\n1. Start with provided initial solution if available\n2. Multi-phase multi-resolution GA:\n   - Phase 1: Binary search space (lower computational cost)\n   - Phase 2: Real-valued search space (higher precision)\n3. Local optimization with gradient-based refinement and continuous SA\n4. Upsampling to finer resolutions\n5. Final repair and verification\n\nFor GA implementation:\n- Population size: 40\n- Crossover: Single-point\n- Mutation: Cauchy-sampled with limiting factor\n- Selection: Tournament (size=3)\n- Constraint repair: Enforce sum while maintaining bounds\n- Objective: Negative cross-correlation maxima\n\nFor local optimization, reuse the existing gradient_refine and continuous_SA implementations.\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- GA variant: (1) Binary, (2) Real-valued\n- Crossover variant: (1) Single-point, (2) Polynomial\n- Mutation variant: (1) Cauchy, (2) Gaussian\n- Temperature schedule for SA: Fast cooling initial, slow final approach\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n- Initial Call: Real-valued GA on 128-points with 300s\n- Binary GA: 256-points with 200s\n- Local refinement: 512-points with gradient SA\n- Final refinement: 1024-points (if time permits)\n- Time monitoring between phases\n- Fallback preparation after each GA phase\n- Constraint handling by random repair if violated\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Verify 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 for all samples\n2. Check sum is exact n_points/2\n3. Compute objective using original correlation method\n4. Final repair re-checks both conditions\n5. Returns h_values in list form (to be converted back to numpy)",
